    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah snapping photos of everything sounds like a lot of work but i guess it's necessary. it just feels so weird though... like i know exactly where i put my receipt, and then five minutes later it's just gone. it's like something is actively hiding them or playing tricks on me. do you think there's a specific psychological reason why people lose small stuff so easily when they're stressed with work?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's very common to misplace small items when you're under the pressure of deadlines, and it's not necessarily a trickster spirit! Psychologically, when we are stressed, our working memory gets overloaded, and our ability to perform routine tasks like \"put this receipt *here*\" suffers. We're operating in a state of high cognitive load, which makes everything seem more slippery than it really is.\n\nThink of your brain like a poorly organized, overcrowded filing cabinet that has too many active demands—it's easier for the small receipts to slip through the cracks when the big projects are demanding all its attention. That's why the immediate capture method is so crucial; it bypasses the need for perfect memory retrieval later on.",
      "source": "target",
